# Cloudflare credentials for THIS project. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   cp .env.example apps/api/.env
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# `wrangler login` stores one OAuth token per machine, in
# ~/Library/Preferences/.wrangler/config/default.toml — shared by every project
# on the computer. Log in to a different Cloudflare account for some other work
# and this project silently starts pointing at that account: `wrangler.toml`
# still pins account_id 4768790a…, the token belongs to someone else, and every
# command fails with "The given account is not valid or is not authorized"
# (D1, code 7403) or a bare "Authentication error" (R2, code 10000). Nothing
# says the cause is that you logged in elsewhere.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN takes precedence over that shared OAuth token, and
# wrangler reads it from a .env in the directory the command runs in. So a token
# here pins this repo to the right account regardless of what the machine-wide
# login is doing.
#
# TWO COPIES, and it is worth knowing why:
#   .env           — the repo root, where `npm run deploy` runs (Pages)
#   apps/api/.env  — where wrangler deploy / d1 / r2 run for the Worker
# wrangler reads the .env of the CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY, and those two
# commands run in different ones. A symlink works if you prefer one file.
#
# Both paths are already in .gitignore — verified, not assumed. Never commit it.
#
# MAKING THE TOKEN
# Cloudflare dashboard → My Profile → API Tokens → Create Token → Custom token,
# restricted to the Indiefluence account, with these permissions:
#
#   Account │ D1                     │ Edit    the content database
#   Account │ Workers R2 Storage     │ Edit    the photograph bucket
#   Account │ Workers Scripts        │ Edit    deploying the API Worker
#   Account │ Cloudflare Pages       │ Edit    deploying the app
#   Account │ Account Settings       │ Read    wrangler resolves the account
#
# Scope it to one account. A token with access to every account you own is the
# same failure mode this file exists to prevent, only quieter.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# Optional. wrangler.toml already pins account_id, so this is only needed if you
# ever run a wrangler command outside a directory that has one.
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=4768790aa47814f60dd70c187c7a7bd9
